<h1>Alexi Giannoulias</h1>
<p>Illinois Secretary of State. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>Secretary of State since 2023 and state treasurer before that; 50, born in Chicago to Greek immigrants, married with four daughters. Filed about 60,000 signatures on the first day and leads the field in money and in the one public poll.</p>
<p><a href="/directdemocracy/app/candidate/wsDeB2jwTGR6XgX96uoOsG2y3hm2">Full platform, open to comments</a></p>
<p><a href="https://alexiforchi.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (alexiforchi.com)</a></p>
<h2>What sets them apart</h2>
<p>Only Giannoulias: A $22 minimum wage by 2032, up from $17, and a law making new data centers pay for the power they use.</p>
<p>Taxes: Hold the city&#39;s property tax levy flat and have the wealthy pay a bigger share. Kelly and Kitover want no new taxes.</p>
<p>Pensions: Merge the four pension funds and offer voluntary buyouts. Quigley and Cardenas would have workers share the cost.</p>
<p>Children: Free full-day preschool for 3- and 4-year-olds, with the state. Cardenas also proposes universal pre-K.</p>
<h2>Where they split from the field</h2>
<p>Property taxes: Hold the city&#39;s levy flat. Freeze or lower them: Kelly, Holberg, and Kitover. An increase is not off the table: Nee.</p>
<p>New taxes: Make the wealthy pay more, with Johnson. No new taxes: Kelly and Kitover. Cut taxes: Wilson. Savings first, before any new revenue: Quigley.</p>
<p>Pensions: Restructure the funds and pay ahead, with Mendoza. Workers share the cost: Quigley and Cardenas. Change the state constitution: Wilson. Bankruptcy on the table: Kitover.</p>
<p>Money for households: A $22 minimum wage. $3,000 a year to working families: Cardenas. A guaranteed income: Ma.</p>
<h2>In their words</h2>
<ul>
<li>A $22 minimum wage by 2032, up from $17. <q>Chicago should never become a city reserved only for the wealthy,</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/illinois-secretary-of-state-alexi-giannoulias-campaign-chicago-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, Aug. 2, 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Hold the city&#39;s property tax levy flat and have the wealthy pay a bigger share. <q>Glittery possible revenue is not responsible.</q> (<a href="https://www.nprillinois.org/chicago-il/2026-08-03/joining-the-chicago-mayors-race-giannoulias-casts-himself-as-a-coalition-builder" rel="nofollow noopener">NPR Illinois, Aug. 3, 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Merge the four city pension funds and offer voluntary buyouts.</li>
<li>Make new data centers pay the full cost of the power they use, and offer free full-day preschool for 3- and 4-year-olds.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committee: Citizens for Giannoulias</li>
<li>Raised: $3.1M</li>
<li>Spent: $6.0M</li>
<li>Cash on hand: $19.2M</li>
<li>Raised July 1 to Sept. 30, 2026; spent Jan. 1 to Sept. 30, 2026. Most of the third quarter&#39;s $5.8 million went to paid media; the committee also reported $2.3 million in debts. <a href="https://elections.il.gov/CampaignDisclosure/CommitteeDetail.aspx?id=19323" rel="nofollow noopener">Source</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>24.9% of likely voters in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC). 4.2 points of support per $1 million spent.</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Would hold the city&#39;s property tax levy flat and grow the economy instead of raising rates, consolidate the four pension funds, and cut what he calls waste and bureaucracy. Warns against counting on revenue that may not come.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>People want competence. People want to know that someone is there that will get things done, that will cut bureaucracy, that will reduce waste, that will make government easier.</q> (<a href="https://abc7chicago.com/amp/post/illinois-sec-state-alexi-giannoulias-officially-announces-bid-mayor-chicago/19615117/" rel="nofollow noopener">ABC7 Chicago, Aug. 2, 2026</a>)</li>
<li><q>Glittery possible revenue is not responsible.</q> (<a href="https://www.nprillinois.org/chicago-il/2026-08-03/joining-the-chicago-mayors-race-giannoulias-casts-himself-as-a-coalition-builder" rel="nofollow noopener">NPR Illinois, Aug. 3, 2026</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>No position on record.</p>
<h2>Also on the record</h2>
<ul>
<li>Played professional basketball in Greece for Panionios in 1998 and 1999, and was once headlined as Obama&#39;s basketball pal. (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexi_Giannoulias" rel="nofollow noopener">en.wikipedia.org</a>)</li>
<li>Says his mother worked at Sears and his father sold pickles seven days a week. (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/illinois-secretary-of-state-alexi-giannoulias-campaign-chicago-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">cbsnews.com</a>)</li>
</ul>
